# mica-required kernel options — the board-independent runtime baseline, shared
# by every board (boards/common/, referenced as the buildx named context
# `mica-common`). Board kernels merge this before olddefconfig and assert every
# =y line against the final .config. dm-verity no-initramfs boot cannot load
# modules, so these must be built-in =y, never =m. Board-specific requirements
# (storage controller, etc.) belong in the board's own config, not here.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_DM=y
CONFIG_DM_INIT=y
CONFIG_DM_VERITY=y

# --- the kernel authenticates the root hash itself --------------------------
#
# WHAT THE FOUR LINES BELOW BUY. Today the verity root hash is a literal on the
# kernel command line: whoever writes the command line decides what the root
# is, and the kernel checks only that the blocks hash to the value it was
# handed. With ROOTHASH_SIG the kernel additionally verifies a detached PKCS#7
# signature over that hash against certificates compiled INTO it, so a root
# hash is authenticated before a single block is mapped. That is the property
# plan 20260908-1428 section 2 needs in order to publish a rootfs without
# rebuilding a kernel or a bootloader for every release.
#
# ENABLING THE SYMBOL DOES NOT REQUIRE A SIGNATURE, which is the half that is
# easy to miss: upstream still permits an unsigned mapping unless
# dm_verity.require_signatures=1 is in force. The symbol is the capability; the
# policy is a boot parameter, and the boot path is what has to carry it.
#
# TRUSTED_KEYS IS A PATH, NOT A KEY, and it is relative to the kernel source
# tree, which is where each board's build stages the file from the `mica-trust`
# named build context (VERITY_TRUST_CERT, the public certificate the assembly
# supplies, staged by common/trust/stage.sh). PUBLIC MATERIAL ONLY: an
# X.509 certificate goes in; the private half stays with the assembly. Keeping the
# path constant and the contents an input is what lets a production build embed
# a production anchor without editing this floor. The string is invisible to
# the =y loops every board runs over this file, so each board asserts it by
# name the way it already asserts the LSM list.
#
# THE COST, stated because it is not free: the kernel image now depends on the
# certificate's bytes. Two trees with different meta/verity/ produce different
# kernels from identical source, so the byte-for-byte rebuild property
# RFCT-343 established holds within a tree and across a shared anchor, not
# across developers who each minted their own.
#
# LOOP IS HERE FOR THE SAME PLAN. It is already =y on all three boards today,
# by inheritance rather than by decision; the file-based A/B design maps the
# root from a FILE on a filesystem, so a loop device stops being a debugging
# convenience and becomes the block device the root is built on.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_LOOP=y
CONFIG_DM_VERITY_VERIFY_ROOTHASH_SIG=y
CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYRING=y
CONFIG_SYSTEM_TRUSTED_KEYS="certs/mica-verity-anchor.pem"
CONFIG_SQUASHFS=y
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_ZSTD=y
# Without XATTR support a squashfs root silently drops file capabilities and
# security xattrs (e.g. Debian's cap_net_raw on ping).
CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XATTR=y
CONFIG_OVERLAY_FS=y

# DATA project quotas protect the state and metadata reserve from bulk writers.
CONFIG_QUOTA=y
CONFIG_QFMT_V2=y

# Inherited from the baseline as "machined-era options
# (harmless) + WireGuard", and retained deliberately: nothing in the shipping
# image requires them. A missing hugetlbfs put the board in a reboot loop on
# hardware (2026-08-17). SELinux is permissive: CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP=y
# and no shipping process changes that.
CONFIG_HUGETLBFS=y
CONFIG_HUGETLB_PAGE=y
# tracefs has NO Kconfig symbol of its own — fs/tracefs is built by
# CONFIG_TRACING (selected by the baseline's FUNCTION_TRACER). The line below
# is an assertion only.
CONFIG_TRACING=y
CONFIG_AUDIT=y
CONFIG_SECURITY=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_NETWORK=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX=y
CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_DEVELOP=y
# SELinux must also be in the LSM boot list or selinuxfs never registers
# (compiled-but-inactive); string option, not covered by the =y assertion loop.
CONFIG_LSM="landlock,lockdown,yama,loadpin,safesetid,integrity,selinux,bpf"

# Fleet remote-management tunnel (systemd-networkd native netdev support).
CONFIG_WIREGUARD=y

# Product requirements, not vendor defaults. Both are already =y in
# the cx3576 vendor config (kernel-cx3576z.config) and BRIDGE is
# additionally asserted in the board Dockerfile; stating them HERE makes the
# shared, per-board-asserted floor the thing that guarantees them, so a vendor
# config regenerated from upstream cannot drop either one silently.
CONFIG_VLAN_8021Q=y
CONFIG_BRIDGE=y

# Container networking (podman/netavark) -- an engine fact, not a board fact,
# so it is floor, not per-board configuration: netavark creates the
# container/host veth pair and programs `fib daddr type local` in an inet
# table, which needs the fib expression for both address families and their
# shared core. The per-symbol citations into the pinned netavark source live
# in tests/netavark-kernel-config-test.sh; the same set was measured
# missing on hardware (s905x5m, cx3576 baseline) as the reason bridge
# networking failed. uefi-x64 runs Debian's kernel, where these ship as modules --
# verify/src/checks-kernel.ts asserts that off the built image.
CONFIG_VETH=y
CONFIG_NFT_FIB=y
CONFIG_NFT_FIB_IPV4=y
CONFIG_NFT_FIB_IPV6=y
CONFIG_NFT_FIB_INET=y

# eBPF runtime. An engine fact, like the netavark block above: crun programs
# the cgroup v2 device controller as a BPF_PROG_TYPE_CGROUP_DEVICE program
# (crun 1.29.1 src/libcrun/ebpf.c:490,496 and cgroup-systemd.c:1482,1501), and
# on cgroup v2 that program IS the device policy -- there is no devices
# controller file to write instead. Debian's kernel sets all four =y, so this
# is a floor both boards can hold. The per-symbol table is in
# docs/boards/contract.md section 4.
CONFIG_BPF=y
CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL=y
CONFIG_BPF_JIT=y
CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF=y

# Firewall: the kernel side of an nftables front-end, with iptables-nft as the
# reference because that is the surface Debian's iptables package provides.
# The citations into iptables 1.8.11 and the per-symbol table are in
# docs/boards/contract.md section 4. Deliberately NOT here: the legacy
# IP_NF_* back-end, which the block below measures unreachable through anything
# the image selects. (Symbol names in these comments
# are written without the CONFIG_ prefix on purpose: merge_config.sh greps this
# whole file for each symbol it merges, and a comment naming one is reported as
# part of that symbol's value.)
CONFIG_NF_TABLES=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_INET=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV4=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_IPV6=y
CONFIG_NFT_COMPAT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XTABLES=y
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK=y
CONFIG_NFT_CT=y
CONFIG_NF_NAT=y
CONFIG_NFT_NAT=y
CONFIG_NFT_MASQ=y

# The x_tables extensions the shipped iptables cannot do without, and the
# measurement that moved them here from "policy". RFCT-304 has the full run.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED, on the uefi-x64 kernel this tree builds (6.12.107, booted under
# QEMU on an initramfs carrying the image's own iptables 1.8.11 and nftables
# 1.1.3), reading nft --json rather than nft list ruleset. That distinction is
# the whole reason this block exists: the TEXT renderer runs an xt expression
# back through libxtables' xlate callback and prints the translation, so a
# compat rule prints as `masquerade` or `fib daddr type local` exactly as a
# native one does. Read from --json instead, EVERY extension the front-end was
# asked for was stored as an `xt` expression -- MASQUERADE, REDIRECT, DNAT,
# CHECKSUM, CT, MARK, addrtype, conntrack. Only the plain verdict and the
# builtin matches (-p, --dport, -m mark) translate natively.
#
# nft_compat resolves that expression through xt_request_find_target/_match,
# which loads the xt module by name. With the module absent the rule is
# REFUSED, and it says so:
#
#   # iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p tcp --dport 80 -j REDIRECT --to-ports 8080
#   Warning: Extension REDIRECT revision 0 not supported, missing kernel module?
#
# so REDIRECT, CHECKSUM and CT -- absent on uefi-x64, =y on cx3576 -- were the
# operator-visible half of the split PLAN-074 section 7h recorded: the
# documented compatibility path worked on one board and refused on the other.
# The other four were =m on uefi-x64 and loaded on demand from the root, so that
# half was cosmetic, which is what the measurement was there to decide rather
# than assume.
#
# WHY =y AND NOT =m, given none of these is needed before the root is mounted.
# Two statements are what this file exists to stop: a floor that said "=m is
# enough here but not there" would be per-board configuration wearing the
# shared file's name, and uefi-x64's config is built by SUBTRACTION, so a symbol
# this file does not pin is actively removed rather than merely defaulted. The
# cost is measured and small -- see docs/boards/contract.md section 4.2.
#
# THIS IS A CHOSEN SUBSET AND CANNOT BE ANYTHING ELSE, and the boundary is
# drawn where it is for a reason that is worth stating exactly, because a
# tidier-sounding one would be false. Any of the roughly sixty xt extensions an
# operator names needs its own module and the image will not carry all of them.
# These eight are the symbols the operator-visible split was MADE of: six of
# them are PLAN-074 section 7h's list once its five legacy tables leave by the
# other route, MATCH_CONNTRACK is here because cx3576's board loop stopped
# restating it and something had to keep the assertion, and XT_NAT is the same
# =y-there/=m-here split as TARGET_MASQUERADE beside it -- not on section 7h's
# list, which was derived from what an earlier floor swap dropped rather than
# from what the front-end needs, and found by running the rules. Any nat
# compatibility path worth the name answers -j DNAT, so promoting MASQUERADE
# and leaving SNAT/DNAT one class weaker would have left the defect open.
#
# NOT "everything both boards already had". Three of the eight -- CHECKSUM, CT
# and REDIRECT -- were absent on uefi-x64, so adopting them ADDED capability there.
# The line is the assigned split, not a cost-free one.
#
# FOUR MORE DIFFERENCES OF THIS EXACT CLASS EXIST AND ARE DELIBERATELY NOT
# CLOSED HERE (RFCT-304, docs/boards/contract.md section 4.2.3): -m multiport,
# -m comment and -j CT --zone answer on cx3576 and are refused on uefi-x64, and -j
# LOG answers on uefi-x64 and is refused on cx3576. Each of those needs a DIRECTION
# chosen -- add the capability to the board that lacks it, or take it from the
# board that has it -- and that is a decision about what the product's
# compatibility path guarantees, not a measurement. Adding a line here is a
# floor change with a size to quote, not a default.
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MARK=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_NAT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_ADDRTYPE=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_CONNTRACK=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_CHECKSUM=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_CT=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_MASQUERADE=y
CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_REDIRECT=y

# What netavark itself needs on top of the front-end above, and the reason both
# lines are stated here rather than in one board:
#
#   NF_CONNTRACK_MARK -- netavark sets and matches a connection mark on the
#   dnat path (src/firewall/nft.rs:286,1090 at the v2.1.0 this tree pins), so
#   published ports depend on it. It is a plain bool that defaults to n under
#   NETFILTER_ADVANCED, so a config that does not name it does not get it: uefi-x64
#   silently had `is not set` while cx3576 asserted it in its own board loop,
#   which is the board-specific floor this file exists to stop.
#
#   NF_NAT_MASQUERADE -- selected by the masquerade expression above, so on
#   today's kernel it is already y. It is named anyway because a select is a
#   fact about 6.12's Kconfig, not a requirement this tree states, and
#   tests/netavark-kernel-config-test.sh asserts that every symbol netavark
#   needs is named by a floor that survives olddefconfig on EVERY board. An
#   unstated symbol that happens to be selected is exactly the shape of the
#   gap above.
#
# Both were on cx3576's board loop until now; that loop no longer restates
# them.
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK_MARK=y
CONFIG_NF_NAT_MASQUERADE=y

# Bridge filtering. podman puts every container's veth on one Linux bridge, and
# traffic between two containers on that bridge is switched at layer 2 -- it
# never reaches the ip-family hooks, so a host firewall cannot see it. These
# three are what make it visible, and nothing below them is: the per-expression
# bridge modules and the legacy ebtables front-end are policy and are left out
# (ebtables-nft does not need the legacy symbol; it programs the bridge family
# through the nf_tables compat expression above, iptables 1.8.11
# iptables/nft.c:898 NFPROTO_BRIDGE -> xtables_bridge).
#
# WHAT AN OPERATOR OBSERVES, and it is not the same on both boards. br_netfilter
# defaults every one of its call-iptables/ip6tables/arptables switches to 1
# (linux net/bridge/br_netfilter_hooks.c:1255-1257), and its hooks register once
# a bridge exists. Built in, as it is on an in-tree board kernel, that happens
# at the first bridge; as a module, as on Debian's, only once something loads
# it. So on cx3576 an ip-family FORWARD policy applies to same-bridge container
# traffic from the moment podman creates its network, and on uefi-x64 today it does
# not. The capability is common; the default state is not, and a DROP policy
# written against one board will not behave the same on the other.
CONFIG_BRIDGE_NETFILTER=y
CONFIG_NF_TABLES_BRIDGE=y
CONFIG_NF_CONNTRACK_BRIDGE=y

# --- CAPABILITY WITHOUT A CONSUMER, and the only block here that is one ------
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A LINE TO IT. Every other symbol in this file names
# something in the image that consumes it -- a reconciler, netavark, crun,
# systemd. These three name nothing. No package in any resolution opens a
# dm-crypt device, the image ships no cryptsetup, no LUKS header is formatted,
# and nothing is encrypted at rest after this lands. docs/design/storage.md
# still marks encryption unsupported and docs/design/security-model.md section
# 6 still says at-rest encryption proceeds only through its own approved plan.
# This is the KERNEL side, provisioned ahead of the decision above it.
#
# WHY AHEAD, and this is the whole argument. A kernel symbol costs a rebuild of
# every board, and uefi-x64's kernel is now configured by subtraction: PLAN-074's
# config drops the driver classes and filesystems nothing consumes, so a symbol
# that is not named here is not merely absent, it is actively removed. Debian
# shipped the crypt target as a module and the trimmed uefi-x64 config does not.
# Naming it now costs one rebuild that was happening anyway; naming it later
# costs two.
#
# HOW THE BLOCK LEAVES. Each line exits when a consumer lands in the image, and
# moves into that consumer's justification -- at which point it is an ordinary
# floor entry like every other. If the product decision goes the other way the
# whole block is deleted. A block with no exit condition becomes permanent by
# accident, so this is the condition.
#
# WHAT IS DERIVED, AND FROM WHAT. LUKS2's default cipher is aes-xts-plain64, so
# the crypt target asks the crypto API for xts(aes): the XTS template and an
# AES implementation. plain64 is generated inside the target and names no
# symbol. SHA256 is already required above for dm-verity, and LUKS2's argon2
# key derivation is cryptsetup's, in userspace, naming no kernel symbol.
#
# THREE MORE SYMBOLS ARRIVE THAT ARE NOT NAMED HERE, and they were found by
# regenerating the config rather than predicted. The crypt target `select`s the
# ESSIV template in 6.12, so it comes in whether or not this block asks for it
# -- the reasoning that it is only needed for the LUKS1 aes-cbc-essiv:sha256
# default is true and irrelevant, because the dependency is the kernel's, not
# ours. The accelerated x86 AES likewise selects the cryptd and SIMD helpers.
# Six built-in symbols in total on uefi-x64, measured, not three.
#
# GENERIC ONLY, DELIBERATELY. The accelerated AES is a different symbol on each
# architecture -- AES_NI_INTEL on x86, AES_ARM64_CE_BLK on arm64 -- and neither
# exists on the other, so a shared floor cannot name either. Each board config
# carries its own, and both do.
CONFIG_DM_CRYPT=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_XTS=y
CONFIG_CRYPTO_AES=y
